      "writtenOn": "2019-04-10T01:38:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So I was in the process of implementing an lis2mdl_interrupt() but I just realized I don\u0027t think that\u0027s right. The lsm6dsm makes sense. Data is read into the FIFO and an interrupt fires, at this point we want to store the timestamp since it\u0027s the closest to the sensor reading. The lis2mdl doesn\u0027t have a FIFO. So we should take the timestamp at read time. For example:\n\nSensor is running at 10Hz and interrupt fires, then the handler is called X milliseconds later. When the handler runs it performs a read, but the value may not be the one that was there when the interrupt fired depending on when the read took place relative to the sensor\u0027s cycles.\n\nI\u0027ll update this to take the timestamp closer to the read itself, but I think that\u0027s about as accurate as we can get.",
      "parentUuid": "c8ca6921_d106591d",
      "revId": "971332cc926132418bcb33e52015c3427a980bb1",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "71a26990_078632bc",
        "filename": "driver/mag_lis2mdl.c",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 273,
      "author": {
        "id": 1104704
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-04-10T05:29:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Remove this irq_handler from this CL, and start another CL for interrupt handling. We/Intel can use the read() entry point. Having a working CL in the tree will make it easier to test in parallel.\n\nFirst, do you have an interrupt line between the magnetometer and the ISH. If not the case is close.\n\nSecond, once the GPIO is set as an interrupt and enabled in board.c, the [hard] interrupt handler, lis2mdl_interrupt(), will collect the timestamp when the sample are ready - last_interrupt_timestamp.\n\nThis timestamp is very precise, the time at which the motion_sense task is scheduled and irq_handler is called is not. That\u0027s why using __hw_clock_source_read() in irq_hanlder() does not offer much improvement over just using read() entry point.\n\nI agree that without a FIFO, the EC must trigger the motion_sense task with the sensor period, otherwise, we will lose samples. For the magnetometer, it is up to 20ms, it is doable.\nFor a accel or gyro at 200Hz, we only have 5ms, which would be very risky.",
      "parentUuid": "e18871d5_95b03ac5",
      "revId": "971332cc926132418bcb33e52015c3427a980bb1",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
